18. In Ring Confidential Transactions (RingCT), what serves as the "public keys" used inside the ring signature loop for a given input?
a) The raw wallet addresses of the senders from previous transactions
b) The difference obtained by subtracting the input's pseudo output commitment from each prior output commitment in the ring
c) The total sum of all new output commitments created in the transaction
d) The secret blinding factors chosen by the recipient of the transaction   
Correct: b)
Explanation:
For each member in an input's ring, the system subtracts the input's pseudo output commitment
from that prior output commitment. These resulting differences act as the "public keys" of the
ring signature. For the one real spent output in the ring, the committed amounts match and cancel 
out, allowing the sender to complete the ring signature loop without revealing which ring member 
was actually spent. 

